---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="TruVector — A checkpoint for AI actions"
  description="TruVector is a checkpoint an AI system runs before it acts. It checks where the information came from, whether the sources are really separate, and whether they agree, then answers Allow, Review, or Block with the reasons attached."
  path="/"
>

  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-line">
    <div class="hero__veil" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="suite suite--flagship hero__inner">
      <p class="kicker">A checkpoint for AI actions</p>
      <h1 class="hero-line" id="hero-line">Your AI is about to act on something it read. TruVector checks it first.</h1>
      <hr class="rule--champagne" />
      <p class="hero-support">TruVector sits between an AI system and the action it&rsquo;s about to take. It checks where the information came from, whether the sources are really separate, and whether they agree. Then it answers: go ahead (Allow), have a person look (Review), or stop (Block). Every answer comes with its reasons, so someone can check it later.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/technology">How it works</a>
        <a class="btn btn--secondary" href="/use-cases">Where it fits</a>
        <a class="btn btn--tertiary" href="/investors">Investor overview <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="room" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">The problem</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="problem-title">AI systems now act on information nobody checked.</h2>
      <div class="flagship reveal" style="margin-top:48px">
        <div class="flagship__pane">
          <p class="lede">AI used to just write things. Now it does things: answers customers, approves requests, places orders, files records, publishes posts.</p>
          <p>The risk isn&rsquo;t only that an AI says something wrong. It&rsquo;s that it <em>acts</em> on something wrong, and afterward nobody can tell what it was relying on.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flagship__pane">
          <p>Most AI tools work on producing better answers. Almost nothing sits between the information and the action and leaves a record someone can review.</p>
          <p>TruVector is built for that gap. It doesn&rsquo;t write content, it doesn&rsquo;t declare what&rsquo;s true for the world, and it doesn&rsquo;t trust a source just because it&rsquo;s a big name.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room room--alt" id="how" aria-labelledby="how-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">What it checks</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="how-title">Three questions before every action.</h2>
      <ul class="rail reveal" style="margin-top:44px">
        <li>
          <span class="rail__name">Where did this come from?</span>
          <span class="rail__note">Every piece of information the AI is relying on is traced back to its source, with when and where it appeared.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="rail__name">Are these really separate sources?</span>
          <span class="rail__note">Fifty pages repeating one wire story are one source, not fifty. Copies are linked back to the original and counted once.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="rail__name">Do the sources agree?</span>
          <span class="rail__note">When sources conflict, the conflict stays visible in the answer instead of being averaged away.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal" style="margin-top:36px;max-width:62ch">The answer is <strong>Allow</strong>, <strong>Review</strong> or <strong>Block</strong>, weighed against what the action would cost if the information turned out to be wrong. It works like a credit check, except it checks the information an AI is about to rely on instead of checking a borrower.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room" id="not" aria-labelledby="not-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">Boundaries</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="not-title">Three things TruVector is not.</h2>
      <ul class="rail reveal" style="margin-top:44px">
        <li>
          <span class="rail__name">Not a chatbot or content writer</span>
          <span class="rail__note">It produces no content of its own. It checks the information other systems are about to act on.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="rail__name">Not a fact-checking service</span>
          <span class="rail__note">It doesn&rsquo;t rule on whether a statement is true. It measures how well the information behind an action holds up, and shows where sources conflict.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="rail__name">Not a truth machine</span>
          <span class="rail__note">It tells you whether the information behind one specific action is solid enough for what that action costs, with its uncertainty stated.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room room--alt" id="company" aria-labelledby="company-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">Company</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="company-title">Built by an engineer who measured things for a living.</h2>
      <div class="flagship reveal" style="margin-top:44px">
        <div class="flagship__pane">
          <p class="lede">Built by Michael Brandon Lane.</p>
          <p>InTellMe is an independent research and software company founded and operated by Michael Brandon Lane, a chemical engineer and chemist based in Johnson City, Tennessee.</p>
          <p>Before InTellMe, Lane worked at Celgard on lithium-ion battery separator and coating technology, including the development and scale-up of coated separator systems for high-volume manufacturing. He is a named inventor on multiple Celgard patents covering coated battery separators, including U.S. Patent 11,949,124.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flagship__pane">
          <p>That work shaped how he builds software: every number should trace back to a measurement, uncertainty should be stated out loud, and conclusions should change when the data does.</p>
          <p>Chemical kinetics describes how a reaction moves: its rate, its direction, and the forces that speed it up or slow it down. Lane Vector applies the same kind of math to how information and intent move between people and machines. TruVector puts it to work checking the information behind an AI&rsquo;s action before the action happens.</p>
          <p class="caption" style="margin-top:22px">Company: <a href="https://www.intellmeai.com/" rel="noopener">InTellMe</a> &middot; The science: <a href="https://lanevector.com/" rel="noopener">Lane Vector</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="room" id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
    <div class="suite suite--flagship">
      <p class="kicker">Contact</p>
      <h2 class="suite-title reveal" id="contact-title">Talk to the person who built it.</h2>
      <p class="reveal" style="margin-top:20px;max-width:56ch">Technical questions, research correspondence and partnership conversations all reach the same inbox, and the reply comes from the engineer.</p>
      <div class="actions reveal" style="margin-top:36px">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/investors#request">Request materials</a>
        <a class="btn btn--secondary" href="mailto:brandon@intellmeai.com">brandon@intellmeai.com</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
